    description: "Found while moving elixir's ipc_send rows (WI-nuhor / WI-busam, branch jgstern-agent/fix/audit0021-rows). After that branch, elixir has NO ipc_send row at all: every one it had was a BEAM send or spawn (now process_send) or the community Oban enqueue (now db_write). The elixir boundary-coverage test (test_io_boundary.py::TestLoadCatalog::test_elixir_catalog_covers_all_expected_boundaries) was asserting ipc_send was present because of those misfiled rows; it now asserts process_send.\n\nElixir's REAL ipc_send surface -- data written to the external program behind an open port, a channel the program set up (audit-findings 0021's definition) -- is misfiled or missing:\n- elixir.yaml `Port: [open, command]` is under `subprocess`. Port.open launches (subprocess is right for it); Port.command(port, data) writes data to the stdin of a program ALREADY running -- the INV-babiz shape (NSTask.terminate / Process.terminate were moved out of subprocess for the same reason). Read at the shipped rows; not measured on a corpus.\n- erlang.yaml has no row for erlang:port_command/2,3 or port_connect; `Port ! {self(), {command, Data}}` is spelled through erlang:send, which is process_send.\n\nEffect: today Port.command is a `subprocess` sink (zone subprocess), so a host_secret -> ipc claim cannot see it, and a must_not_exist subprocess claim reports a launch where none happens. erlang:port_command is invisible.\nFix shape: Port.command -> ipc_send (mirroring python Popen.stdin.write / rust ChildStdin rows), add erlang:port_command, with a reach fixture each; keep Port.open under subprocess. Check whether Port.command should be `simultaneous` with anything before moving (it launches nothing)."  # b61b
    fields: {}  # b61b
